Explanation:
Paragraph 1 introduces and formalizes a new problem, dynamic necklace splitting, and presents several linear-time algorithms for solving it. This paragraph creates significant original scientific value by presenting novel methods and findings. Therefore, I assign this segment the highest percentage of importance at 35%.

Paragraph 2 introduces another NP-complete problem (MinNodeMaxFlow) and provides an approximation algorithm for special cases. It also mentions using these algorithms as part of a larger batch relocation algorithm when n=2 with optimal cuts. This paragraph creates original scientific value by presenting new problems, proofs, and algorithms. I assign this segment the second-highest percentage at 25%.

Paragraph 3 presents two linear-time algorithms for general necklace splitting (n≥2) that achieve an optimal bound of n(k−1)cuts. It also mentions adapting these algorithms for efficient insertion and deletion. This paragraph creates original scientific value by presenting new methods and findings, but it builds upon the work introduced in Paragraph 1. I assign this segment a percentage of importance at 20%.

Paragraph 4 presents a randomized polylogarithmic-time algorithm for relocation, insertion, and deletion that produces an approximately fair set of cuts with high probability when n=2. This paragraph creates original scientific value by presenting new methods and findings but builds upon the work introduced in Paragraph 1. I assign this segment a percentage of importance at 15%.

Paragraph 5 serves as a summary or comparison table for the algorithms presented in previous sections, without introducing any new knowledge itself. This paragraph consumes and repackages knowledge created elsewhere in the paper. Therefore, it creates no original scientific value on its own. I assign this segment the lowest percentage of importance at 5%.

The percentages sum to 100 as required.
